The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    dput(cifs_sb->root);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `cifs_kill_sb` function releases cached dentries (including the root dentry) even when the filesystem mount is incomplete, which can happen during mount failures. If the mount is not complete, `cifs_sb->root` may not be properly initialized, leading to dereferencing invalid pointers and causing instability or crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    node = rb_first(root);
    while (node != NULL) {
        ...
    }
    dput(cifs_sb->root);
    cifs_sb->root = NULL;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that cached dentries are only released if the filesystem mount is complete, indicated by the presence of a valid `cifs_sb->root`. This prevents the dereferencing of uninitialized or invalid pointers during mount failures, thus stabilizing the CIFS filesystem handling.

